Joins
2 tables k data ko ek sath show krwany ko joins kehty ha

INNER JOIN (Simple Explanation)
INNER JOIN is used to combine two tables based on a common column. It only returns rows where there is a match in both tables. If a row in one table doesn't have a matching row in the other table, it is excluded from the result.

Think of it like a guest list and an attendance list at a party. If you only want people whose names appear on both lists, you use an INNER JOIN. People who are on the guest list but didn't attend, or people who attended but weren't on the guest list, are not included.

Visual Example:-

Students Table
student_id	name
1	        John
2	        Mary
3	        Bob

Projects Table
student_id	marks
1	         85
2	         90
4	         88	← Student 4 doesn't exist in Students

INNER JOIN Query
SELECT students.name, projects.marks
FROM students
INNER JOIN projects ON students.student_id = projects.student_id;

Result:
name	marks
John	85
Mary	90
Notice:

✅ John and Mary appear because they have matches in both tables
❌ Bob is excluded because he has no project
❌ Student 4 is excluded because they are not in the Students table

Key Takeaway 📝
INNER JOIN = "Show me only the data that exists in BOTH tables." If something is missing from either side, it doesn't appear in the results.
------------------------------------------------------------
LEFT JOIN (Simple Explanation)
LEFT JOIN returns all rows from the left table, and only the matching rows from the right table. If there's no match in the right table, the result will show NULL values for the right table's columns.

Think of it like a guest list (left table) and an attendance list (right table). With LEFT JOIN, you want to see everyone on the guest list, whether they attended or not. If they attended, you see their attendance details. If they didn't attend, you still see their name but with empty (NULL) attendance information.

Visual Example
Students Table (Left)
student_id	name
1	        John
2	        Mary
3	        Bob

Projects Table (Right)
student_id	marks
1	         85
2	         90
4	         88

LEFT JOIN Query
SELECT students.name, projects.marks
FROM students
LEFT JOIN projects ON students.student_id = projects.student_id;

Result
name	marks
John	85
Mary	90
Bob	NULL	← Bob has no project, but still appears!

Notice:
✅ John and Mary appear with their marks (matches found)
✅ Bob appears with NULL because he has no matching project
❌ Student 4 is NOT shown because they are not in the left table

RIGHT JOIN (Simple Explanation)
RIGHT JOIN returns all rows from the right table, and only the matching rows from the left table. If there's no match in the left table, the result will show NULL values for the left table's columns.

Think of it like an attendance list (right table) and a guest list (left table). With RIGHT JOIN, you want to see everyone who attended, whether they were on the guest list or not. If they were on the guest list, you see their details. If they weren't, you still see their attendance but with empty (NULL) guest information.

Visual Example

Students Table (Left)
student_id	name
1	        John
2	        Mary
3	        Bob

Projects Table (Right)
student_id	marks
1	         85
2	         90
4	         88

RIGHT JOIN Query
SELECT students.name, projects.marks
FROM students
RIGHT JOIN projects ON students.student_id = projects.student_id;

Result
name	marks
John	85
Mary	90
NULL	88	← Student 4 has project but not in students!

Notice:
✅ John and Mary appear with their marks (matches found)
✅ Student 4 appears with NULL for name (no matching student)
❌ Bob is NOT shown because he has no project in the right table

Quick Comparison Table 📊 
JOIN Type	What You Get	                             Example
INNER JOIN	Only rows that match in BOTH tables	     Show students who have projects
LEFT JOIN	ALL rows from left + matches from right	     Show ALL students, with project details if they have any
RIGHT JOIN	ALL rows from right + matches from left	     Show ALL projects, with student details if they have any

The Easiest Way to Remember 🧠
LEFT JOIN = Left table is Loyal → All left rows stay, even without matches
RIGHT JOIN = Right table is Reserved → All right rows stay, even without matches
INNER JOIN = Only the INtersection → Only common ground

Key Takeaway 📝
LEFT JOIN = "Show me everything from the left table, and only matching data from the right. If there's no match, show NULL."
RIGHT JOIN = "Show me everything from the right table, and only matching data from the left. If there's no match, show NULL."
INNER JOIN = "Show me only what exists in BOTH tables. If something is missing from either side, don't show it."
-----------------------------------------------------
FULL JOIN (Simple Explanation)
FULL JOIN (also called FULL OUTER JOIN) returns ALL rows from BOTH tables. It combines the results of both LEFT JOIN and RIGHT JOIN. If a row exists in the left table but not the right, it shows NULLs for the right table's columns. If a row exists in the right table but not the left, it shows NULLs for the left table's columns.

Think of it like two guest lists for a party. A FULL JOIN shows you:
✅ People who are on both lists (matches)
✅ People who are only on the first list (left-only)
✅ People who are only on the second list (right-only)
Everyone appears, regardless of whether they're on one list or both!

Visual Example
Students Table (Left)
student_id	name
1	         John
2	         Mary
3	         Bob

Projects Table (Right)
student_id	marks
1	         85
2	         90
4	         88
5	         95

FULL JOIN Query
SELECT students.name, projects.marks
FROM students
FULL JOIN projects ON students.student_id = projects.student_id;

Result
name	marks
John	85
Mary	90
Bob	NULL	← Bob has no project
NULL	88	← Student 4 has project but no name
NULL	95	← Student 5 has project but no name

Notice:
✅ John and Mary appear with their marks (matches)
✅ Bob appears with NULL for marks (left-only)
✅ Student 4 and 5 appear with NULL for name (right-only)
✅ ALL 5 students/projects are shown!

Important: FULL JOIN in MySQL ⚠️
FULL JOIN is NOT directly supported in MySQL!

If you try this in MySQL:
 ❌ This will NOT work in MySQL!
SELECT students.name, projects.marks
FROM students
FULL JOIN projects ON students.student_id = projects.student_id;
Error: MySQL doesn't recognize FULL JOIN!

How to Do FULL JOIN in MySQL (Workaround)
Since MySQL doesn't support FULL JOIN, you can simulate it using LEFT JOIN + RIGHT JOIN + UNION:
Method 1: UNION of LEFT and RIGHT JOIN
LEFT JOIN (students with or without projects)
SELECT students.name, projects.marks
FROM students
LEFT JOIN projects ON students.student_id = projects.student_id

UNION
RIGHT JOIN (projects with or without students)
SELECT students.name, projects.marks
FROM students
RIGHT JOIN projects ON students.student_id = projects.student_id;

Key Takeaway 📝
FULL JOIN = "Show me EVERYTHING from BOTH tables - students with projects, students without projects, AND projects without students. Everyone is included!"
In MySQL: Since FULL JOIN isn't supported, use LEFT JOIN UNION RIGHT JOIN to get the same result
