The "Two-Step" Strategy for "Top/MAX" Problems

It's Like Saying:
"Who got the highest score in Math?"

Wrong way: "Give me all Math scores, take the highest, and also give me a random name" ❌

Right way: "First find the highest Math score (97), then find who got 97 in Math" ✅

Problem:
Find the top scorer in every subject.
Steps:
Whenever you need "the top X in each group," use this mental model:

Example
Step 1: Find the "Target Value"
sql
-- Find the maximum score for each subject
SELECT subject, MAX(score) 
FROM exam_scores 
GROUP BY subject;
Step 2: Find "Who Achieved It"
sql
-- Find the student(s) who got those scores
SELECT s.name, e.subject, e.score
FROM students s
INNER JOIN exam_scores e ON s.student_id = e.student_id
WHERE (e.subject, e.score) IN (
    -- Put Step 1 here
    SELECT subject, MAX(score)
    FROM exam_scores
    GROUP BY subject
);
